WEBVTT

00:07.230 --> 00:09.370
We introduce OTRetarget,

00:09.410 --> 00:14.020
a method that jointly retargets robot and object motion via optimal transport.

00:14.690 --> 00:18.060
From a human demonstration, we read the interactions off the surfaces,

00:18.100 --> 00:19.900
transport them onto the robot,

00:19.940 --> 00:23.330
and solve for robot and object poses together.

00:23.870 --> 00:26.470
Everything begins with a person moving.

00:26.980 --> 00:29.250
We start from a human demonstration.

00:29.950 --> 00:32.206
We recover the body mesh and the object poses,

00:32.246 --> 00:35.650
which gives us surfaces to work with, not just joints.

00:36.800 --> 00:40.500
So the first step is to describe the interaction with geometry,

00:40.540 --> 00:42.400
not with joints.

00:43.710 --> 00:47.420
So we sample points densely on every surface.

00:47.460 --> 00:51.670
For each point, and each surface near it, we store three things:

00:51.710 --> 00:55.810
how far it is, signed, so penetration comes out negative;

00:56.350 --> 00:59.610
where the closest point on that surface lies;

00:59.650 --> 01:01.141
and which way it faces,

01:01.181 --> 01:05.030
because distance alone can't tell you which side of a thin plate you are on.

01:06.660 --> 01:09.660
Next, we need a correspondence between the two bodies.

01:09.700 --> 01:11.640
Those triples live on the human.

01:11.680 --> 01:13.728
To use them, we need to know which part of the robot

01:13.768 --> 01:16.280
plays the role of which part of the human.

01:16.320 --> 01:18.280
Hand to hand, foot to foot.

01:18.620 --> 01:21.270
Entropic optimal transport gives us that,

01:21.310 --> 01:26.180
once, in a T-pose, for every pair of human part and robot link.

01:26.460 --> 01:28.870
Marginal constraints leave nothing behind:

01:29.150 --> 01:32.930
14% of extremal points unmatched, versus 42.

01:33.950 --> 01:37.760
The same correspondence also works between objects.

01:38.780 --> 01:42.940
Because it is purely geometric, one capture gives many.

01:42.980 --> 01:46.247
We can scale the scene, or swap the box for a ball,

01:46.287 --> 01:49.480
a torus, a beam, and keep the interaction.

01:49.760 --> 01:52.390
Then everything goes into one solver.

01:52.930 --> 01:54.940
The demonstration sets the targets.

01:54.980 --> 01:57.980
One program balances style against interaction,

01:58.020 --> 02:00.060
joint limits and non-penetration.

02:00.100 --> 02:02.330
One QP per frame, warm-started.

02:02.370 --> 02:06.330
Robot and object poses are solved together, so the object stays reachable.

02:06.370 --> 02:12.440
Against OmniRetarget, robot–object interaction goes from 28 to 87 percent,

02:12.480 --> 02:15.290
with roughly a third of the error everywhere else.

02:15.330 --> 02:18.303
These references train a whole-body tracking policy,

02:18.343 --> 02:20.230
which we run on a real G1.
